# Complete internal preparation before paid review

Authority: user direction, 2 October 2026: human review costs US$500–1,200 per
hour; do everything feasible internally first to minimise that bill. This
changes the work order, not the accounting/evidence standard or mission scope.
MC01–MC34 remain in force; the new user direction is retained as MC35. Relevant immediate requirements are MC02, MC04–05,
MC19, MC23–MC27 and MC29–MC34; G2, G7–G10 and G12–G18.

## Objective and boundaries

Remove avoidable reviewer work and uncertainty before commissioning any
professional review. Produce tested internal findings, source-specific
accounting dossiers and a queue separating unfinished internal work from
specific residual professional judgments. Do not use an accountant to debug
software, fetch routine sources, reconcile arithmetic, repair exposition or
discover missing inputs. Do not call AI checks independent accounting assurance.

The current authorised campaign implements this rule and performs the first
substantive preparation pass. The full compiler, richer sector engines and
new real-company semantic models remain internal work; none becomes an expert
question merely because it has not been implemented yet. A paid engagement is
not ready while its dependent internal work remains open. Continue authorised
internal work without waiting for human feedback.

## Work and evidence

1. Preserve the delivered Version 1.1 sources, checks, mission and manifests
   before altering the preparation workflow. Keep the original repair report
   as a historical result, not a claim that newer files retain its hashes.
2. Audit all residual G categories for who can resolve them and the next
   discriminating artifact. Make the internal-first rule persistent in mission
   control, project instructions, requirements and checkpoint.
3. Implement a bounded semantic validator over the existing debt IR and its
   frozen external registry. Check reference identity/kind, expression types
   and cycles, exact domains, dates, review/evidence admissibility, opening
   balance, contract bounds, assumptions and package locks. Unsupported node
   kinds must return a scoped unsupported result, not pass silently.
4. Challenge it with deliberately corrupted inputs that still satisfy the
   structural schema: wrong currency, fake references, cycles, invalid dates,
   noncanonical rationals, impossible domains, omitted rationale, future
   evidence, stale package bytes and invalid contract parameters. Positive
   results must exercise actual fixture data. Passing this validator is not
   full source compilation, dynamic execution or accounting validity.
5. Recheck the local primary paragraphs used for inventory, compound bonds and
   short-duration insurance. Build small source/assumption/calculation dossiers
   with exact local anchors, competing readings, applicability limitations and
   proposed narrow review questions. Keep source-acquisition and missing
   implementation tasks on the internal queue. Do not repeatedly retry known
   blocked sources without a distinct lead.
6. Record findings and repairs, executed commands and precise coverage limits.
   Create a future review brief with scope, supplied evidence, explicit
   deliverables, a time cap to be chosen by the user and stop/clarification
   terms. It is an unsent draft, not an engagement or approval request.

## Skeptical audit before execution

| Risk | Correction |
| --- | --- |
| “Human pending” becomes a reason to stop | Treat it as acceptance status only. Allocate all feasible source, calculation, code and document repairs to internal work first. |
| Claiming savings from more AI output | No hours or dollars saved are estimated without an actual comparator. User-supplied rates show only the price of a possible time budget. |
| Adding a giant engine to avoid a small expert decision | Build a bounded validator that can falsify the existing contract. Keep later software tasks separate from accounting judgment; no full-runtime completion claim. |
| Schema-only checks repeat the old proxy | Use semantically corrupted, schema-valid inputs and exact rejection targets. Report unsupported coverage explicitly. |
| Self-review becomes certification | Label author/code/source checks accurately. Reserve professional judgment for identified applicability or interpretation issues after preparation. |
| Source age hidden by a precise quotation | Record edition, jurisdiction, inspected paragraph and unresolved current-version applicability separately. No full ASC/ASBE authority is inferred from secondary/company material. |
| A cheap-looking review hides preparation costs | Report actual work and known limits; no invented ROI or universal optimal-hour threshold. Ask for a quote/cap only on a concrete ready packet and only when commissioning is requested. |
| Endless preparation | Stop this pass when the bounded checks, source dossiers and complete triage are reviewable. Keep remaining internal work explicit; no claim of readiness for paid review. |

Decision: execute. The pass compares the current candidate against its own
documented semantic requirements. The primary criterion is repaired,
reproducible findings and a correctly separated residual-work queue. Test
counts, paper counts and reviewer-hour scenarios are descriptive only. Failures
trigger local repair; unresolved domain applicability blocks that claim's
promotion, not continued preparation elsewhere. No outside party is contacted
and no professional fees are authorised by this plan.

## Findings requiring a manuscript correction

The semantic check exposed a Real-valued integer slot limit and missing stress
and contract-rate assumption records. The local source inspection found a
Caterpillar bibliography/ledger anchor to Note 7; inventories are Note 8. The
insurance case needs explicit one-year eligibility and recognition timing for
the reinsurance group, separately from the issued contracts. Repair these in
the authored generators and manuscript, then rebuild and inspect changed pages.
Keep the delivered Version 1.1 and its render evidence intact; issue Version
1.2 with a new preparation manifest. This is a correction within the authorised
scope, not a new professional engagement.

The AASB 132/17 compilations apply to annual periods beginning on/after
1 January 2023 and before 1 July **2026**, as checked in the PDFs. A date in a
recovered session summary had incorrectly said July 2023 for AASB 132; that
summary is not source authority. The period-start condition also means these
editions are not automatically inapplicable just because today's date is after
July 2026. Current-jurisdiction amendment checks remain separate internal work.
